{
  "about": "The challenge a passkey signs for a step of the owner's (a command, a resume, a move, a statement of the encryption keys): sha256 of signed_bytes, which are the ASCII proofpane-step/1, a zero byte, and the step's canonical JSON (RFC 8785) in UTF-8. The page makes it (ui/src/passkey.ts, stepChallenge; challenges.test.ts), the server checks it (passkeys.step_challenge in check_command; test_signing_domains.py) and the link client checks it (ade_link.step_challenge in _verify_assertion; test_signing_domains.py), and all three are held to this file. In older releases a step's challenge was the sha256 of its canonical JSON alone (old_challenge here): the page no longer makes that form and the server and the link client no longer take it, so nothing a passkey signed in an older release, a touch over a challenge the server picked included, stands for a step. Computed with Python's hashlib and json alone.",
